  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 327 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA cloudy but much cooler start to the week is underway across \neastern Kansas with below-average temperatures expected for the \nremainder of the work week. Today and Tuesday's conditions will \nlargely be influenced by the large upper low that is progged to \nmoved across Nebraska and Iowa. Ensemble guidance keeps a strong \nsurface high across the northern Plains and Midwest with continuous \nCAA for today and Tuesday. Ample cloud cover and CAA will keep \ntemperatures pleasant and well below-average, topping out in the \nupper 60s. \n\nA subtle pattern change comes Wednesday as the low-level ridge axis \npasses east of Kansas and returns low-level flow back towards the \nsouth. Drier and clearer conditions are expected by the afternoon \nand should promote temperatures returning to the mid to upper 70s. \nBy Wednesday evening into Thursday, rain chances begin to increase \nas vorticity advection on the western side of the upper ridge moves \nnorth into Kansas. Best forcing looks to remain across western and \ncentral Kansas so expectations are for the highest chances for \nrainfall Wednesday night and Thursday to remain there. Thursday into \nFriday will see the upper ridge compress and get shunted back \ntowards the Gulf as troughing approaches from the west. This should \nusher in better PVA across all of eastern Kansas and in turn, rain \nand storm chances. Multiple waves pass over the central Plains into \nthe weekend keeps scattered chances of rain/storms across the \nforecast area with temperatures creeping back towards averages for \nthis time of year.",
